[{:seon.task/name "tests-reaching-finds-the-tests-a-change-selects"
  :seon.task/plan "A cited var's page lists its direct tests (```seon.render.ui/cited-of```: one query, newest first, cut at :seon.config.ctx/callers), and the tests that pin a fix site are often further out: the live-agents' second audit found none on four pages (bytes-str, lead-sentence, cut-words, help), where print_test's cuts-of users reach cut-words 4+ hops out and ref_test's summary test reaches lead-sentence through its callers. A walk over db facts at depth 2 cost 0.1-1.3M ticks a page and found none of them (root, 2026-10-27: not built). ```my.agent/tests-reaching``` answers every test whose reach includes a var, nearest first, each with its last result (```seon.proofs/latest-status```), from the reach graph the test plan reads (```seon.agent/branch-graph```, keyed by the branch's db value) by the reverse walk a change's re-fold takes (```seon.program/reached-by```, ```seon.program/tests-reaching```), leaving out what the plan never selects (```seon.agent/judges?```). Measured on the program's graph: related-view 3 tests in 2k ticks, bytes-str 33 in 15k, lead-sentence 77 in 48k (its summary test 13th), cut-words 856 and print-value 854 in 1.55M (6 ms). The cited render names the form (:reaching). Left: live-agents puts it into the :work and :whole instructions in place of (my.agent/callers 'ns/f) once this merges (seon.repl-test/the-template-instructions-run needs the name to exist). In a process test's harness the root imports no test forms, so it answers [] there; the program-level proof is the equivalence."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/the-tests-reaching-a-var-are-what-its-change-selects seon.ctx-test/a-cited-vars-tests-are-shown-past-its-callers-window}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-outside-agent-proves-its-process-tests-at-the-judge"
  :seon.task/plan "THE PLAN 0.7's first attempt (root, 2026-10-27) found three gaps in proving from an outside agent. (1) A deftest the agent defined at its REPL that reached a process var ran in the agent's fork and errored (\"no process store … bin/test makes it\"): ```seon.agent/run-selection``` ran a process test when its namespace was named or it was a proof (```seon.proofs/left-out```, right for bin/test on a checkout), and a child forked from an agent can never start one. It now leaves every process test to the judge's merge gate, listed in :deferred with :why; the done rule already passes over a deferred proof, and root's gate runs it before a merge. (3) seon_prove answered counts and red names: the server's :prove names each red with its first failing assertion (:failures {test {:message :expected :actual}}) and the deferred tests with why; ```my.agent/prove``` folds what it measured into the observation ```my.task/status``` renders, so a proof's status is not left :unknown. (6) ```my.agent/process-test?``` answers why a test reaches a process on this branch (seon.program/process-path), or nil. Then the blind agent (root, 2026-10-27): a prove naming its proofs (171 green) left both proofs :proof-unknown in status, and a bare prove answered 25k chars. ```my.agent/prove``` folds what it measured into the observation status renders whichever call measured it (a proof's status, and the done rule's reason about it replaced), and answers the whole run printed as its verdict (```my.agent/prove-summary```, the schema :seon.test/proved's :seon.render/summary, the one printer's mechanism): each proof's status, reds with their first assertion, deaths \"died, not counted\", and \"ran N, deferred M, reused K\", the lists being the value under the eval's handle."
  :seon.task/kind :whole
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/an-outside-agents-process-test-goes-to-the-judge-and-a-red-says-why seon.prove-test/an-agents-run-leaves-every-process-test-to-the-judge seon.server-test/a-named-prove-counts-for-status-and-the-judge-and-answers-its-verdict}}]
